A virtual function in C++ is a member function that can be overridden by derived classes in object-oriented programming. This feature allows each class to provide its own implementation of a given function, based on the specific requirements of the derived class. The keyword 'virtual' is used to declare such functions in C++.

The main advantage of virtual functions is polymorphism, which allows objects of a subclass to be treated as objects of their base class, even if the actual type of the object is different. This behavior makes it possible to write more flexible and reusable code, especially in situations where we need to handle multiple types dynamically within our program.

For example:

```cpp
class Shape {
public:
    virtual void draw() = 0; // Pure virtual function
};

class Circle : public Shape {
public:
    void draw() { std::cout << "Drawing a circle\n"; }
};

class Rectangle : public Shape {
public:
    void draw() { std::cout << "Drawing a rectangle\n"; }
};
```

In this example, the `Shape` class has a virtual function `draw`, which is then overridden by both `Circle` and `Rectangle` classes. By creating pointers or references to base class `Shape`, we can call the specific `draw` implementation of either a circle or rectangle without knowing their exact type at compile time:

```cpp
int main() {
    Shape* shapes[2];
    shapes[0] = new Circle();
    shapes[1] = new Rectangle();

    for (auto shape : shapes) {
        shape->draw();
    }
}
```

In the above code, calling `draw()` on each shape in the array will execute the specific implementation of that shape, either drawing a circle or rectangle.

Encapsulation is a fundamental object-oriented programming (OOP) concept that binds the data and functions acting on the data as a single unit, thereby hiding the internal details and complexities of an object from external entities. This principle allows objects to interact with each other only through well-defined interfaces, promoting data privacy, modularity, and ease of code maintenance.

A simple example of encapsulation in Java involves creating a class with private member variables and public getter/setter methods:

```java
public class Account {
    private int accountNumber;
    private String accountHolderName;
    private double accountBalance;

    // Constructor
    public Account(int accountNumber, String accountHolderName, double initialDeposit) {
        this.accountNumber = accountNumber;
        this.accountHolderName = accountHolderName;
        this.accountBalance = initialDeposit;
    }

    // Getter methods (Read-only access to data)
    public int getAccountNumber() {
        return accountNumber;
    }

    public String getAccountHolderName() {
        return accountHolderName;
    }

    public double getAccountBalance() {
        return accountBalance;
    }

    // Setter methods (Write-access to data)
    public void setAccountNumber(int accountNumber) {
        this.accountNumber = accountNumber;
    }

    public void setAccountHolderName(String accountHolderName) {
        this.accountHolderName = accountHolderName;
    }

    // Deposit method to update the account balance
    public void deposit(double amount) {
        accountBalance += amount;
    }

    // Withdraw method to update the account balance
    public boolean withdraw(double amount) {
        if (amount > accountBalance) {
            return false;
        } else {
            accountBalance -= amount;
            return true;
        }
    }
}
```

In this example, the Account class encapsulates the data related to a bank account (account number, holder name, and balance). The private variables make it impossible for other classes or users to directly access these fields, ensuring their privacy. The getter and setter methods provide controlled access to modify the data, while the deposit() and withdraw() methods allow specific actions on the account. This encapsulation makes the Account class easier to work with, test, and maintain, as well as reducing potential errors caused by direct data manipulation.

